*======================================================================
* ed.a99 - the workbench's editor: a full-screen editor for DIS/VAR 80
* source files, up to 800 lines of 80 columns.
*
*   arrows move            FCTN 5 BEGIN start of line    FCTN 6 PROC'D end
*   FCTN 7 AID / 8 REDO    a page up / down
*   type                   inserts (FCTN 2 INS switches to overwriting)
*   ENTER splits the line  CTRL H deletes the character before the cursor
*   FCTN 1 DEL             deletes the character under it
*   FCTN 3 ERASE           deletes the line
*   CTRL S saves           CTRL A saves and assembles
*   CTRL X back to the menu (twice if there are unsaved changes)
*
* The lines are kept in SAMS pages, 51 lines of 80 bytes to a page, in the
* order of a table of slot numbers (so inserting and deleting a line moves
* only the table).  The pages come from the kernel's page pool (PALLOC) as the
* file grows, up to 2,040 lines (40 text pages and one page for the table);
* a table in APPRAM (EDPGT) says which pool page holds each block of 51 slots.
* This file is the guide's example of a program whose data does not fit in
* the memory it can see: read PROGRAMMERS-GUIDE.md, appendix C, with it.
*======================================================================
* THE IDEA OF THIS PROGRAM, for anyone learning to use the SAMS memory.  The
* TI-99/4A's CPU sees only 64K at a time, and about 7K of that is yours
* (APPRAM).  A SAMS card holds up to 16 megabytes, in 4,096-byte PAGES, and
* the kernel lets a program look at any page through a WINDOW: a 4K slice of
* the CPU's address space that the SAMS card can point at any page.  The
* kernel keeps slot A (>2000-3FFF, its own data and your APPRAM) and slot C
* (>C000, the running module) mapped always; windows E and F (>E000-EFFF and
* >F000-FFFF) are yours to point at pages with MAPE and MAPF.  A program with
* more data than fits in APPRAM keeps it in pages and maps the one it needs,
* when it needs it.  That is all this editor does, to hold a file of up to
* 2,040 lines of 80 columns (163,200 bytes) with 7K of RAM.
*
* Three structures, all small:
*   1. TEXT PAGES. Each pool page holds 51 lines of 80 bytes (4,080 of its
*   4,096 bytes). A line lives in a SLOT: slot s is line (s mod 51) of the
*   s/51-th text page. Pages are taken from the kernel's page pool (PALLOC) one
*   at a time as the file grows, so a small file uses few.
*   2. THE PAGE TABLE, EDPGT, in APPRAM: 40 words, one per block of 51 slots,
*   each the number of the pool page that holds that block. It is the only
*   place that says where the text is.
*   3. THE ORDER TABLE, on a pool page of its own (EDORD): word n is the slot
*   of line n. The order of the lines is the order of this table, so inserting
*   or deleting a line moves at most 2,040 words in it and never moves any
*   text.
*
* To find line n (LNPTR): map the order page into window F, read word n (the
* slot s); divide s by 51; the quotient picks a word of the page table (the
* page to map into window E) and the remainder, times 80, is the line's offset
* in the window.  Everything else in the editor (typing, deleting, drawing,
* saving) is built on LNPTR, so none of it needs to know any of this.  Read
* LNPTR, NEWSLOT, INSLINE and DELLINE first, then the screen and key code.
*
* Why a slot table rather than moving the lines?  Insert in the middle of a
* 2,000-line file would otherwise copy up to 160K of text.  This way it copies
* up to 4K of table, which is a tight loop of 2,040 MOVs.  Real editors use
* the same trick (a 'gap buffer' or a line table).
       COPY "kernel.equ"
       COPY "macros.a99"

* The editor's variables, all in APPRAM.  Notice the way they are declared:
* EQU gives each a fixed address; nothing is reserved or cleared by the
* assembler.  The areas used here (APPRAM+128 to +255, and +744 to +823)
* belong to the editor by agreement with the shell (wb.a99), the assembler
* (asm.a99) and the program launcher; the table in docs/WORKBENCH.md part 5
* says who owns what.  Because they live in APPRAM they keep their values when
* you leave the editor for the menu and come back: that is how the buffer
* survives a trip to the assembler.
* storage (the shell's area: APPRAM+128 up to +255)
SRCNAME EQU APPRAM             the file name (the shell's)
EDN    EQU  APPRAM+128         lines in the buffer
EDTOP  EQU  APPRAM+130         the line at the top of the screen
EDCUR  EQU  APPRAM+132         the cursor's line
EDCOL  EQU  APPRAM+134         the cursor's column (0-79)
EDINS  EQU  APPRAM+136         non-zero: typing inserts
EDDIRT EQU  APPRAM+138         unsaved changes
EDFREE EQU  APPRAM+140         the next never-used slot
EDNFR  EQU  APPRAM+142         slots on the free list
EDLOAD EQU  APPRAM+144         non-zero: a buffer is loaded (its name in EDNAME)
EDSURE EQU  APPRAM+146         CTRL X was pressed once with unsaved changes
EDT    EQU  APPRAM+148         scratch words (8)
EDOLD  EQU  APPRAM+248         the cursor's cell on screen (-1: none)
EDDRAW EQU  APPRAM+250         0 nothing, 1 this line, 2 everything to draw
EDMSG  EQU  APPRAM+252         a message to show instead of the help (0: none)
* the loaded file's name (32 bytes: 31 and a length)
EDNAME EQU   APPRAM+164
EDNPG  EQU  APPRAM+196         text pages taken from the pool so far
EDORD  EQU  APPRAM+198         the pool page that holds the order table
EDFL   EQU  APPRAM+200         the free list: 16 slots
EDPOOL EQU  APPRAM+232         non-zero: the pool has been set up (PINIT)
EDTRUNC EQU APPRAM+234         non-zero: the file was too long to load whole
EDPGT  EQU  APPRAM+744         40 words: the pool page of each block of slots
MAXLN  EQU  2040               lines: 40 pages of 51
MAXPG  EQU  40
LPP    EQU  51                 lines (slots) to a page: 51 * 80 = 4,080 bytes

* Colours: attribute bytes built from ROLES (see examples/hello for the
* explanation).  ACUR is the cursor cell: background and foreground are
* exchanged so that the cursor shows as a filled block in the accent colour.
ANORM  EQU  (RTEXT*16+RBG)*256
ABAR   EQU  (RBARFG*16+RBARBG)*256
AMARK  EQU  (RMARK*16+RBG)*256
AERR   EQU  (RERROR*16+RBG)*256
ACUR   EQU  (RBG*16+RACCNT)*256

* The module entry table (see examples/hello): INIT, TICK, REDRAW.  The shell
* reaches this module by GOMOD PG_ED, which maps its code page at >C000 and
* calls INIT.
       AORG >C000
       DATA INIT,TICK,REDRAW,0

* INIT - from the shell: load the file unless it is the one already here.
INIT   .enter
       BL   @POOLUP            the pool and the table's page, once
       BL   @SAMENAME
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  IN1
       BL   @LOADFILE
IN1    CLR  @EDSURE
       BL   @REDRAW
       .leave
       RT

* Why the editor sets the pool up itself: the kernel never calls PINIT for
* you.  The pool is a bitmap of free pages kept in the kernel's own memory;
* PINIT says which range of pages it manages and marks them all free.  The
* workbench's own code pages (PGFREE and PGFREE+1) and the two pages a program
* under test is run from (PGUSER = PGFREE+2, +3) lie below the range, so the
* pool never hands them out.  NPAGES is the size of the card as the kernel
* found it at start (128 pages for 512K, up to 4096 for 16M), so the pool
* grows with the card.
*
* EDPOOL is the flag that says 'done'.  The shell clears it (and EDLOAD) when
* it runs a program, because a program may PINIT the pool its own way and the
* editor's pages would then not be reserved any more; the editor sets
* everything up afresh the next time it is entered.  PALLOC returns the lowest
* free page, or 0 when there is none (page 0 is the kernel's, so 0 is never a
* real result).
* POOLUP - the first time (and after a program has been run, which may have
* used the pool in its own way): the pool manages every page from the first
* one above the workbench's own (PGFREE+4) to the end of the card, and the
* editor takes one page for its order table.  Uses R0-R6.
POOLUP .enter
       MOV  @EDPOOL,@EDPOOL
       JNE  PU9                already done
       LI   R0,PGFREE+4        (PGFREE, +1: the workbench's pages; +2, +3:
       MOV  @NPAGES,R1         PGUSER, the program being run)
       BL   @PINIT
       SETO @EDPOOL
       CLR  @EDNPG             no text pages yet
       CLR  @EDLOAD            and so no buffer
       BL   @PALLOC            a page for the order table
       MOV  R0,@EDORD
PU9    .leave
       RT

* SAMENAME - R0 = 1 if a buffer is loaded and its name is SRCNAME's.
SAMENAME
       CLR  R0
       MOV  @EDLOAD,@EDLOAD
       JEQ  SN9
       CLR  R1
       MOVB @SRCNAME,R1
       SRL  R1,8
       INC  R1
       LI   R2,SRCNAME
       LI   R3,EDNAME
SN1    CB   *R2+,*R3+
       JNE  SN9
       DEC  R1
       JNE  SN1
       LI   R0,1
SN9    RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* the buffer
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* How a line is found.  The call BL @LNPTR with R1 = n leaves R2 = the
* address, in window E, of line n's 80 bytes.  Read it as three lookups: order
* table (window F) gives the slot; the quotient and remainder of slot / 51
* give the block and the place in the block; the page table gives the pool
* page of the block, which MAPE puts in window E.
*
* Two TMS9900 points worth knowing.  DIV and MPY work on register PAIRS: DIV
* R3,R4 divides the 32-bit number in R4:R5 by R3, leaving the quotient in R4
* and the remainder in R5, so R4 must be cleared first (the high half of the
* dividend) and R5 must hold the slot; MPY R3,R2 multiplies R2 by R3 and
* leaves the 32-bit product in R2:R3, so the low half, the one we want, is in
* R3.  And the code addresses a word of a table with @>F000(R1): the address
* >F000 plus the register, which must be a BYTE offset (hence SLA R1,1 to turn
* a line number into a word offset).
*
* MAPE and MAPF change the SAMS card's registers for the window and keep the
* kernel's record (CURBE, CURBF) true, which is what makes it safe for other
* kernel code to borrow a window and give it back.  Never write the card's
* registers yourself.
* LNPTR - R1 = a line number: R2 = the address of its 80 bytes (its page
* mapped at >E000).  Uses R0-R5.
LNPTR  .enter
       MOV  R1,@EDT
       MOV  @EDORD,R0          the order table's page into window F ...
       BL   @MAPF
       MOV  @EDT,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  @>F000(R1),R5      ... the line's slot
       CLR  R4
       LI   R3,LPP
       DIV  R3,R4              R4 = the block of slots, R5 = the place in it
       SLA  R4,1
       MOV  @EDPGT(R4),R0      the pool page that holds that block ...
       BL   @MAPE              ... into window E
       LI   R3,80
       MOV  R5,R2
       MPY  R3,R2              R3 = place * 80
       AI   R3,>E000
       MOV  R3,R2
       .leave
       RT

* How a new line gets storage.  A slot is either on the FREE LIST (slots given
* back by deleted lines, kept in EDFL, 16 of them), or never used (the numbers
* from EDFREE up).  Taking from the free list first reuses pages that are
* already in the table.  When a never-used slot falls in a block we have no
* page for (the slots the pages we hold can hold are EDNPG * 51), a new page
* is taken from the pool by NEWPAGE.  A -1 result means the buffer is full:
* either 2,040 lines, or no free page left in the pool.
* NEWSLOT - R0 = an unused slot (from the free list, else the next never
* used), or -1 if the buffer is full.  Uses R0, R1.
NEWSLOT .enter
       MOV  @EDNFR,R1
       JEQ  NS1
       DEC  R1                 a slot on the free list: take the last
       MOV  R1,@EDNFR
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  @EDFL(R1),R0
       JMP  NS9
NS1    MOV  @EDFREE,R0         else the next never-used slot ...
       CI   R0,MAXLN
       JL   NS2
       SETO R0                 ... unless the buffer is full
       JMP  NS9
NS2    MOV  @EDNPG,R2          the slots the pages taken so far hold
       LI   R3,LPP
       MPY  R3,R2              R3 = pages * 51
       C    R0,R3
       JL   NS3                the slot is in a page we have
       BL   @NEWPAGE           else take another page from the pool
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  NS3
       SETO R0                 the pool is empty: full
       JMP  NS9
NS3    INC  @EDFREE
       MOV  @EDFREE,R0
       DEC  R0                 the slot just taken
NS9    .leave
       RT

* NEWPAGE - take a page from the pool for the next block of 51 slots and
* note it in EDPGT.  R0 = the page, or 0 if there is none (or the table is
* full).  Uses R0-R5.
NEWPAGE .enter
       MOV  @EDNPG,R1
       CI   R1,MAXPG
       JHE  NP8
       BL   @PALLOC            R0 = a page, 0 if the pool is empty
       MOV  R0,R0
       JEQ  NP9
       MOV  @EDNPG,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  R0,@EDPGT(R1)
       INC  @EDNPG
       JMP  NP9
NP8    CLR  R0
NP9    .leave
       RT

* A deleted line's slot goes on the free list if there is room; if the list is
* full the slot is simply forgotten, which wastes 80 bytes until the file is
* loaded again and is far cheaper than keeping a long list.  Reloading resets
* EDFREE, so every slot is used again.
* FREESLOT - give slot R0 back (if the list has room).  Uses R1.
FREESLOT
       MOV  @EDNFR,R1
       CI   R1,16
       JHE  FS9
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  R0,@EDFL(R1)
       INC  @EDNFR
FS9    RT

* BLANKLN - R2 = a line's address: 80 spaces.  Uses R1, R2, R3.
BLANKLN LI  R1,>2020
       LI   R3,40
BL1    MOV  R1,*R2+
       DEC  R3
       JNE  BL1
       RT

* The order table is a plain array of words in window F.  To open a gap at
* line n the loop moves every word from the end back to n up by one place
* (copying from the top downwards, so nothing is overwritten before it is
* copied: the usual rule for an overlapping move).  Then the new line's slot
* goes into the gap and the line count goes up.  The text itself is never
* moved.
* INSLINE - insert a blank line before line number R1 (R1 = EDN adds at the
* end).  R0 = 0 done, -1 the buffer is full.  The new line is current in
* window E at R2.  Uses R0-R5.
INSLINE .enter
       MOV  R1,@EDT+2
       BL   @NEWSLOT
       CI   R0,-1
       JEQ  IL9
       MOV  R0,@EDT+4          the slot
       MOV  @EDORD,R0
       BL   @MAPF
       MOV  @EDN,R3            shift the table up from the end
       SLA  R3,1
       AI   R3,>F000
       MOV  @EDT+2,R4
       SLA  R4,1
       AI   R4,>F000
IL1    C    R3,R4
       JLE  IL2
       MOV  @-2(R3),*R3
       DECT R3
       JMP  IL1
IL2    MOV  @EDT+4,*R4
       INC  @EDN
       MOV  @EDT+2,R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       BL   @BLANKLN
       MOV  @EDT+2,R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       CLR  R0
IL9    .leave
       RT

* Deleting is the same move in the other direction (copying from the bottom
* upwards), after giving the line's slot back.  The file always keeps at least
* one line, so deleting the only line blanks it instead.
* DELLINE - remove line R1 (the last line is blanked instead).  Uses R0-R5.
DELLINE .enter
       MOV  R1,@EDT+2
       MOV  @EDN,R0
       DEC  R0
       JNE  DL1
       MOV  @EDT+2,R1          the only line: blank it
       BL   @LNPTR
       BL   @BLANKLN
       JMP  DL9
DL1    MOV  @EDORD,R0
       BL   @MAPF
       MOV  @EDT+2,R4
       SLA  R4,1
       MOV  @>F000(R4),R0
       BL   @FREESLOT
       MOV  @EDT+2,R4
       SLA  R4,1
       AI   R4,>F000
       MOV  @EDN,R3
       SLA  R3,1
       AI   R3,>F000
DL2    MOV  R4,R0
       INCT R0
       C    R0,R3
       JHE  DL3
       MOV  *R0,*R4
       INCT R4
       JMP  DL2
DL3    DEC  @EDN
DL9    .leave
       RT

* The file service (FOPEN, FREAD, FWRITE, FCLOSE) works on any device the
* console can reach, so the same code reads a disk, a TIPI, or a CF7+.  Each
* call changes R6 to R9 (it runs the disk controller's own code, which uses
* those registers), so anything the loop needs across a call is kept in
* memory: EDT+6 here, EDT+14 in SAVEFILE.
*
* A record read is copied into the line and the rest of the line stays blank
* (BLANKLN was done by INSLINE).  Records longer than 80 are cut.  Reading
* goes on until the file service reports end of file; since each loop turn
* inserts the line before reading it, the last line (never filled) is deleted
* at the end.  If the buffer fills first (INSLINE answers -1) the load stops,
* EDTRUNC is set and a message says so: SAVEFILE then refuses, so that a
* partly loaded file can never be saved over the whole one.
* LOADFILE - read SRCNAME into the buffer (an empty buffer of one blank
* line if the file is not there).  Uses everything.
LOADFILE .enter
       CLR  @EDN
       CLR  @EDTOP
       CLR  @EDCUR
       CLR  @EDCOL
       CLR  @EDDIRT
       CLR  @EDFREE            (the pages already taken are used again)
       CLR  @EDNFR
       CLR  @EDTRUNC
       SETO @EDINS
       LI   R1,SLOADG          'Loading DSK1.NAME ... lines' on row 29
       CLR  R2
       BL   @PRGINI
       LI   R2,SRCNAME
       LI   R0,FIN+FVAR
       LI   R1,80
       BL   @FOPEN
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  LF8
LF1    MOV  @EDN,R1
       MOV  R1,R0              every 8 lines the count and the turning bar
       ANDI R0,7
       JNE  LF1A
       BL   @PRGUP
       MOV  @EDN,R1
LF1A   BL   @INSLINE
       CI   R0,-1
       JEQ  LF7
       MOV  R2,@EDT+6          the line's address
       LI   R1,EDRBUF
       BL   @FREAD
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  LF6
       MOV  @EDT+6,R3          the record, then spaces, into the line
       LI   R1,EDRBUF
       CI   R2,80
       JLE  LF2
       LI   R2,80
LF2    MOV  R2,R4
       JEQ  LF4
LF3    MOVB *R1+,*R3+
       DEC  R4
       JNE  LF3
LF4    JMP  LF1
LF6    MOV  @EDN,R1            end of file: the last line was never filled
       DEC  R1
       BL   @DELLINE
       JMP  LF7C
LF7    SETO @EDTRUNC           the buffer filled before the file ended:
       LI   R0,SLONG           say so, and SAVEFILE will refuse (saving
       MOV  R0,@EDMSG          would cut the file on the disk)
LF7C   BL   @FCLOSE
LF8    MOV  @EDN,R0
       JNE  LF9
       CLR  R1
       BL   @INSLINE
LF9    LI   R1,SRCNAME         remember the name
       LI   R2,EDNAME
       CLR  R3
       MOVB *R1,R3
       SRL  R3,8
       INC  R3
LF10   MOVB *R1+,*R2+
       DEC  R3
       JNE  LF10
       SETO @EDLOAD
       .leave
       RT
EDRBUF EQU  APPRAM+5500        a record being read or written (256 bytes)

* Saving writes each line as one record with its trailing blanks cut (the
* editor stores lines padded to 80, files store them as long as they are).
* The loop counter EDT+14 and the lengths are in memory, not registers,
* because FWRITE clobbers R6-R9.  A write error closes the file and returns
* its code; otherwise the dirty flag is cleared.
* SAVEFILE - write the buffer to SRCNAME, trailing blanks cut.  R0 = 0 or
* the error.  Uses everything.
SAVEFILE .enter
       MOV  @EDTRUNC,@EDTRUNC
       JEQ  SVOK
       LI   R0,>00FE           a file that was only partly loaded: not saved
       JMP  SV9
SVOK   LI   R1,SSAVG           'Saving DSK1.NAME ... lines of n' on row 29
       MOV  @EDN,R2
       BL   @PRGINI
       LI   R2,SRCNAME
       LI   R0,FOUT+FVAR
       LI   R1,80
       BL   @FOPEN
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  SV9
       CLR  @EDT+14            (the file service clobbers R6-R9)
SV1    MOV  @EDT+14,R1         every 8 lines the count and the turning bar
       MOV  R1,R0
       ANDI R0,7
       JNE  SV1A
       BL   @PRGUP
SV1A   MOV  @EDT+14,R9
       C    R9,@EDN
       JHE  SV8
       MOV  R9,R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       MOV  R2,R3              cut the trailing blanks
       AI   R3,80
SV2    C    R3,R2
       JLE  SV3
       DEC  R3
       CB   *R3,@SPACE
       JEQ  SV2
       INC  R3
SV3    S    R2,R3
       MOV  R3,@EDT+8          the length
       LI   R4,EDRBUF
       MOV  R3,R5
       JEQ  SV5
SV4    MOVB *R2+,*R4+
       DEC  R5
       JNE  SV4
SV5    LI   R1,EDRBUF
       MOV  @EDT+8,R2
       BL   @FWRITE
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  SV7
       INC  @EDT+14
       JMP  SV1
SV7    MOV  R0,@EDT+10
       BL   @FCLOSE
       MOV  @EDT+10,R0
       JMP  SV9
SV8    BL   @FCLOSE
       CLR  @EDDIRT
       CLR  R0
SV9    .leave
       RT
SPACE  BYTE >20
       EVEN

* The progress line.  A file read or written a record at a time on a floppy
* takes seconds, so row 29 says what is going on: 'Loading DSK1.NAME   n lines'
* with a bar that turns, or 'Saving DSK1.NAME   n lines of N' (the buffer's
* size is known when saving).  Written every 8 records, which costs next to
* nothing beside the disk, and cleaned off when the screen is drawn again.
* PRGINI - the line begun: R1 = the verb (a string), R2 = the number of lines
* to come (0: not known).  Uses R0-R7.
PRGINI .enter
       MOV  R2,@EDT+12
       .push R1
       LI   R0,29*COLS
       LI   R1,>2000
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLN
       LI   R0,29*COLS
       LI   R1,ABAR
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLA
       .pop R1
       LI   R0,29*COLS+1
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,29*COLS+9
       LI   R1,SRCNAME
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,29*COLS+49
       LI   R1,SLINES
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       MOV  @EDT+12,R1
       JEQ  PI9
       LI   R0,29*COLS+58
       LI   R1,SOF
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,29*COLS+61
       MOV  @EDT+12,R1
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @DECW
PI9    CLR  R1
       BL   @PRGUP
       .leave
       RT

* PRGUP - the count R1 and the next turn of the bar.  Uses R0-R7.
PRGUP  .enter
       .push R1
       LI   R0,29*COLS+44
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @DECW
       .pop R1
       SRL  R1,3
       ANDI R1,3
       AI   R1,SPIN
       MOVB *R1,R1
       LI   R0,29*COLS+55
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTC
       .leave
       RT
SPIN   BYTE >7C,>2F,>2D,>5C    | / - \

* PRGEND - the line taken off again.  Uses R0-R2.
PRGEND .enter
       LI   R0,29*COLS
       LI   R1,>2000
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLN
       LI   R0,29*COLS
       LI   R1,ANORM
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLA
       .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* The screen is Text 80 mode, 80 columns by 30 rows: row 0 the status bar,
* rows 1 to 28 the text, row 29 the help line.  A line is copied to the screen
* with VMBW (a block write to VRAM, the video chip's own memory); the kernel's
* text routines hide the video chip's address registers, and keep the screen
* consistent with the attribute table, so the editor never talks to the video
* chip directly.
* the screen: row 0 the status, rows 1-28 the text, row 29 the help
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
REDRAW .enter
       SETO @EDOLD
       LI   R1,ANORM
       BL   @CLS
       BL   @DRAWALL
       .leave
       RT

DRAWALL .enter
       BL   @FIXTOP
       CLR  R9
DA1    BL   @DRAWROW
       INC  R9
       CI   R9,28
       JL   DA1
       BL   @STATUS
       BL   @CURSOR
       .leave
       RT

* FIXTOP - scroll so that the cursor's line shows.  Uses R0.
FIXTOP MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       C    R0,@EDTOP
       JHE  FT1
       MOV  R0,@EDTOP
       RT
FT1    AI   R0,-27
       C    R0,@EDTOP
       JGT  FT2                (signed: the cursor may be near the top)
       RT
FT2    MOV  R0,@EDTOP
       RT

* DRAWROW - screen row R9 (0-27): its line, or blanks.  Uses R0-R5.
DRAWROW .enter
       MOV  R9,R3
       INC  R3
       LI   R4,80
       MPY  R4,R3              R4 = the row's first cell
       MOV  @EDTOP,R1
       A    R9,R1
       C    R1,@EDN
       JHE  DR2
       MOV  R4,@EDT+12
       BL   @LNPTR
       MOV  R2,R1
       MOV  @EDT+12,R0
       LI   R2,80
       BL   @VMBW
       JMP  DR9
DR2    MOV  R4,R0
       LI   R1,>2000
       LI   R2,80
       BL   @FILLN
DR9    .leave
       RT

* STATUS - the bar on row 0, and the help line or a message on row 29.
STATUS .enter
       CLR  R0
       LI   R1,ABAR
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLA
       LI   R0,1
       LI   R1,SEDIT
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,7
       LI   R1,SRCNAME
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,40
       LI   R1,SLINE
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,45
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       INC  R1
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @DECW
       LI   R0,50
       LI   R1,SOF
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,53
       MOV  @EDN,R1
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @DECW
       LI   R0,60
       LI   R1,SCOL
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,64
       MOV  @EDCOL,R1
       INC  R1
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @DECW
       LI   R0,70
       LI   R1,SOVR
       MOV  @EDINS,@EDINS
       JEQ  ST1
       LI   R1,SINS
ST1    LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       MOV  @EDDIRT,@EDDIRT
       JEQ  ST2
       LI   R0,78
       LI   R1,>2A00           * : unsaved changes
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTC
ST2    LI   R0,29*COLS+1
       LI   R1,SHELP
       MOV  @EDMSG,@EDMSG
       JEQ  ST3
       MOV  @EDMSG,R1
ST3    LI   R2,AMARK
       BL   @PUTS
       .leave
       RT

* CURSOR - the cell under the cursor in the accent colour; the old one back.
CURSOR .enter
       MOV  @EDOLD,R0
       JLT  CU1
       LI   R1,ANORM
       LI   R2,1
       BL   @FILLA
CU1    MOV  @EDCUR,R3
       S    @EDTOP,R3
       INC  R3
       LI   R4,80
       MPY  R4,R3              R4 = the row's first cell
       A    @EDCOL,R4
       MOV  R4,@EDOLD
       MOV  R4,R0
       LI   R1,ACUR
       LI   R2,1
       BL   @FILLA
       .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* TICK is called once a frame with the key in R0's high byte.  Printable keys
* (>20 to >7E) go to TYPECH.  Every other key is looked up in KEYTAB, a table
* of pairs: the key's code (a byte) and the address of the routine that
* handles it; the loop walks the pairs until it finds the key or reaches the 0
* that ends the table, and calls the routine with BL *R1 (a call through a
* register).  Adding a key is one line in the table and one routine.  After
* any key the screen is brought up to date: EDDRAW says how much to redraw (0
* nothing, 1 only the cursor's line, 2 everything), which keeps typing fast:
* typing a character redraws one line, not the screen.
* the keys
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
TICK   .enter
       MOV  R0,R9
       JNE  TK0
       B    @TK9
TK0    CI   R9,KQUIT*256
       JNE  TK1
       SETO @QUITF
       B    @TK9
TK1    CLR  @EDMSG
       MOV  @EDTOP,@EDT+10
       CLR  @EDDRAW
       CI   R9,>9800           CTRL X (any other key cancels its confirmation)
       JEQ  TKX
       CLR  @EDSURE
TKX    CI   R9,>2000
       JL   TK2
       CI   R9,>7F00
       JHE  TK2
       BL   @TYPECH
       JMP  TKEND
TK2    MOV  R9,R0              the other keys, by a table
       SRL  R0,8
       LI   R1,KEYTAB
TK3    MOV  *R1,R2
       JEQ  TKEND              the table's end
       C    R2,R0
       JEQ  TK4
       AI   R1,4
       JMP  TK3
TK4    MOV  @2(R1),R1
       BL   *R1
TKEND  BL   @FIXTOP
       C    @EDTOP,@EDT+10
       JEQ  TK5
       LI   R1,2
       MOV  R1,@EDDRAW
TK5    MOV  @EDDRAW,R0
       CI   R0,2
       JNE  TK6
       BL   @DRAWALL
       B    @TK9
TK6    CI   R0,1
       JNE  TK7
       MOV  @EDCUR,R9          this line only
       S    @EDTOP,R9
       BL   @DRAWROW
TK7    BL   @STATUS
       BL   @CURSOR
TK9    .leave
       RT

* the editing keys: key code (the low byte), routine
KEYTAB DATA >00C5,KEYUP,>00D8,KEYDN,>00D3,KEYLT,>00C4,KEYRT
       DATA >000D,KEYENT,>0088,KEYBS,>00F1,KEYDEL,>00F3,KEYERA
       DATA >00F2,KEYINS,>00F5,KEYHOM,>00F6,KEYEND,>00F7,KEYPGU
       DATA >00F8,KEYPGD,>0093,KEYSAV,>0098,KEYEXIT,>0081,KEYASM,0

KEYUP  MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       JEQ  KU9
       DEC  @EDCUR
KU9    RT
KEYDN  MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       INC  R0
       C    R0,@EDN
       JHE  KD9
       MOV  R0,@EDCUR
KD9    RT
KEYLT  MOV  @EDCOL,R0
       JEQ  KL9
       DEC  @EDCOL
KL9    RT
KEYRT  MOV  @EDCOL,R0
       CI   R0,79
       JHE  KR9
       INC  @EDCOL
KR9    RT
KEYHOM CLR  @EDCOL
       RT
KEYPGU MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       AI   R0,-20
       JGT  KP1
       CLR  R0
KP1    MOV  R0,@EDCUR
       RT
KEYPGD MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       AI   R0,20
       C    R0,@EDN
       JL   KP2
       MOV  @EDN,R0
       DEC  R0
KP2    MOV  R0,@EDCUR
       RT
KEYINS INV  @EDINS
       RT

* LINELEN - R1 = a line's length without its trailing blanks; R2 = its
* address (the line must be mapped: LNPTR first).  Uses R1, R3.
LINELEN
       MOV  R2,R3
       AI   R3,80
LL1    C    R3,R2
       JLE  LL2
       DEC  R3
       CB   *R3,@SPACE
       JEQ  LL1
       INC  R3
LL2    MOV  R3,R1
       S    R2,R1
       RT

KEYEND .enter
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       BL   @LINELEN
       CI   R1,79
       JLE  KE1
       LI   R1,79
KE1    MOV  R1,@EDCOL
       .leave
       RT

* Typing in INSERT mode moves the rest of the line one place right, from the
* last column backwards (again so that nothing is overwritten before it is
* copied), before putting the character in.  In overwrite mode (FCTN 2) it
* just replaces.  Every change sets EDDIRT (the '*' in the status bar) and
* asks for the cursor's line to be redrawn.
* TYPECH - the character R9 (high byte) into the line at the cursor.
TYPECH .enter
       MOV  @EDCOL,R0
       CI   R0,80
       JHE  TC9
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       MOV  @EDCOL,R3
       MOV  @EDINS,@EDINS
       JEQ  TC2
       LI   R4,79              slide the rest of the line right
TC1    C    R4,R3
       JLE  TC2
       MOV  R2,R5
       A    R4,R5
       MOVB @-1(R5),*R5
       DEC  R4
       JMP  TC1
TC2    A    R3,R2
       MOVB R9,*R2
       INC  @EDCOL
       SETO @EDDIRT
       LI   R0,1
       MOV  R0,@EDDRAW
TC9    .leave
       RT

* KEYDEL - delete the character under the cursor.
KEYDEL .enter
       BL   @DELCH
       .leave
       RT
DELCH  .enter
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       MOV  @EDCOL,R4
DC1    CI   R4,79
       JHE  DC2
       MOV  R2,R5
       A    R4,R5
       MOVB @1(R5),*R5
       INC  R4
       JMP  DC1
DC2    MOV  R2,R5
       AI   R5,79
       MOVB @SPACE,*R5
       SETO @EDDIRT
       LI   R0,1
       MOV  R0,@EDDRAW
       .leave
       RT

* KEYBS - delete the character before the cursor; at the start of a line,
* join it to the one before if both fit in 80 columns.
KEYBS  .enter
       MOV  @EDCOL,R0
       JEQ  KB1
       DEC  @EDCOL
       BL   @DELCH
       JMP  KB9
KB1    MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       JEQ  KB9
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1          this line's text to EDRBUF
       BL   @LNPTR
       BL   @LINELEN
       MOV  R1,@EDT+4
       LI   R4,EDRBUF
       MOV  R1,R5
       JEQ  KB2
KB1A   MOVB *R2+,*R4+
       DEC  R5
       JNE  KB1A
KB2    MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       DEC  R1
       BL   @LNPTR
       BL   @LINELEN
       MOV  R1,@EDT+6          the previous line's length
       A    @EDT+4,R1
       CI   R1,80
       JH   KB9
       MOV  @EDT+6,R3
       A    R3,R2
       LI   R4,EDRBUF
       MOV  @EDT+4,R5
       JEQ  KB3
KB2A   MOVB *R4+,*R2+
       DEC  R5
       JNE  KB2A
KB3    MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       BL   @DELLINE
       DEC  @EDCUR
       MOV  @EDT+6,@EDCOL
       SETO @EDDIRT
       LI   R0,2
       MOV  R0,@EDDRAW
KB9    .leave
       RT

* KEYENT - split the line at the cursor.
KEYENT .enter
       MOV  @EDN,R0
       CI   R0,MAXLN-1
       JHE  KT9
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1          the tail (from the cursor on) to EDRBUF, blanked
       BL   @LNPTR
       MOV  R2,R3
       A    @EDCOL,R3
       LI   R4,EDRBUF
       LI   R5,80
       S    @EDCOL,R5
       MOV  R5,@EDT+12
KT1    MOVB *R3,*R4+
       MOVB @SPACE,*R3+
       DEC  R5
       JNE  KT1
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       INC  R1
       BL   @INSLINE
       LI   R3,EDRBUF
       MOV  @EDT+12,R5
KT2    MOVB *R3+,*R2+
       DEC  R5
       JNE  KT2
       INC  @EDCUR
       CLR  @EDCOL
       SETO @EDDIRT
       LI   R0,2
       MOV  R0,@EDDRAW
KT9    .leave
       RT

* KEYERA - delete the line.
KEYERA .enter
       MOV  @EDCUR,R1
       BL   @DELLINE
       MOV  @EDCUR,R0
       C    R0,@EDN
       JL   KA1
       MOV  @EDN,R0
       DEC  R0
       MOV  R0,@EDCUR
KA1    CLR  @EDCOL
       SETO @EDDIRT
       LI   R0,2
       MOV  R0,@EDDRAW
       .leave
       RT

* KEYSAV - save.
KEYSAV .enter
       BL   @SAVEFILE
       MOV  R0,@EDT+12
       BL   @PRGEND
       MOV  @EDT+12,R0
       JEQ  KS1
       LI   R1,SSERR
       JMP  KS2
KS1    LI   R1,SSAVED
KS2    MOV  R1,@EDMSG
       .leave
       RT

* KEYEXIT - back to the menu; twice if there are unsaved changes.
KEYEXIT .enter
       MOV  @EDDIRT,@EDDIRT
       JEQ  KX1
       MOV  @EDSURE,@EDSURE
       JNE  KX1
       SETO @EDSURE
       LI   R0,SUNS
       MOV  R0,@EDMSG
       JMP  KX9
KX1    LI   R0,PG_WB
       CLR  R1
       BL   @GOMOD
KX9    .leave
       RT

* KEYASM - save and go to the menu to assemble.
KEYASM .enter
       BL   @SAVEFILE
       MOV  R0,R0
       JEQ  KM1
       LI   R0,SSERR
       MOV  R0,@EDMSG
       JMP  KM9
KM1    LI   R0,PG_WB
       LI   R1,1
       BL   @GOMOD
KM9    .leave
       RT

SEDIT  STRI 'Edit'
SLOADG STRI 'Loading'
SSAVG  STRI 'Saving'
SLINES STRI 'lines'
SLINE  STRI 'line'
SOF    STRI 'of'
SCOL   STRI 'col'
SINS   STRI 'INS'
SOVR   STRI 'OVR'
SHELP  BYTE SHELPE-SHELPB
SHELPB TEXT 'CTRL S save  CTRL A save+assemble  CTRL X menu  CTRL H b'
       TEXT 'ackspace  FCTN 3 line'
SHELPE
SSAVED STRI 'saved                                                          '
SLONG  BYTE SLONGE-SLONGB
SLONGB TEXT 'the file is longer than 2,040 lines: only part is loaded and'
       TEXT ' it cannot be saved'
SLONGE
SSERR  STRI 'the file could not be written                                    '
SUNS   BYTE SUNSE-SUNSB
SUNSB  TEXT 'unsaved changes: CTRL S saves, CTRL X again leaves witho'
       TEXT 'ut saving   '
SUNSE
       EVEN
